      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224516",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $x\u223cy$ if and only if $\\sqrt{3}(x - y)$ is rational number, for $x, y \u2208 R$. Then, $\u223c$ is a transitive relation on $R$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x, y, z \u2208 R$ such that $x\u223cy$ and $y\u223cz$.\n> $L2$: Then, $\\sqrt{3}(x - y)$ and $\\sqrt{3}(y - z)$ are rational numbers.\n> $L3$: So, $\\sqrt{3}(x - z) = \\sqrt{3}(x - y) + \\sqrt{3}(y - z)$ is a rational number.\n> $L4$: Thus, $x\u223cz$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $\u223c$ is transitive on $R$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ by the fact that the {{field-2}} of two {{field-3}} is another {{field-4}}.\n\nTo prove that $\u223c$ is an equivalence relation, we also need to demonstrate that $\u223c$ is {{field-5}} and {{field-6}}."
